Key GRC Principles: Governance


*governance refers to the framework of policies, processes, and rules that guide how an organization is directed, controlled, and held accountable.

*governance occurs at the top-most level of any organization. it is designed to establish the overarching vision, strategic direction, and frameworks that guide the organizations operations and decision-making processes.






Governance Objectives: at the top level in any governance model, leadership must establish organizational goals and objectives. this provides coordination and consistency across the organization and can be a driving factor for organizational focus. objectives set at the highest level can then be integrated into lower-level objectives. high-level objectives do not need to be overly specific on how they will be accomplished, but they must provide enough information of consistent application of more specific guidance. for example: a high-level objective might state that the organization will hire and retain high quality staff. this forms a general mission statement that groups within the organization can use. this specific example likely results in a comprehensive set of policies, procedures, and guidance within the human resources department, but it also informs other business units of managements intent regarding staffing, which is likely to modify the decisions made by all managers within the organization.





Governance Approach: organizations may choose different governance approaches depending on several factors. the most common approaches, however, are to use either a centralized top-down approach, a decentralized delegated approach, or a combination of the two. in a centralized top-down approach, all governance decisions are managed by a central decision-making body. this can be advantageous from a consistency perspective but requires the central decision-making body to be well informed of all organizational elements and activities. conversely, a decentralized delegated approach gives authority to smaller elements of the organization (e.g. departments, divisions) to make governance decisions but risks inconsistencies between elements. many orgs opt for a hybrid approach that centralizes some governance decisions and delegates others. the specific mix of centralized decisions and decentralized decisions depends heavily on the organizational structure. 





Governance Output: once a governance decision has been made, the decision must be promulgated. one of the most common ways of providing governance output is through creation and distribution of policies. policy describes governance decisions in a meaningful and useful way to the organization and can also provide more detailed information to functional areas or divisions within a larger organization. an important element of governance output is distributing governance polices to ensure all members have appropriate awareness and training. 







