Key GRC Principles: Compliance


*sometimes compliance stems from internal needs. for example: Ro is working for a company and the company wants to implement a new standard. because the company is implementing a new standard such as HIPAA, PCI DSS, SOX, etc., the company is self imposing this new standard onto itself. This new standard now has to be checked for Ro's company to ensure its compliant. if Ro's company wanted to implement HIPAA (Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (est. 1996)), and Ro does a compliance audit and sees that the company has a policy that conflicts with HIPAA, HIPAA becomes the new priority and Ro must help mediate by rewriting the company policy to ensure it aligns with the company's new standard (HIPAA).




-----

External Compliance: this refers to government regulations all companies must follow. the government creates a set of rules and standards, usually in line with where the companies operate and what type of work they do. if Ro's company is in the business of selling food products made from recycled shoes. if he ever has customers daring enough to try his recycled "shoe food", the government will impose the necessary regulations intended to ensure Ro's customers know exactly what they are eating, the healthcare Ro's company has been given, etc. this will only happen, when and if the government approves Ro's business operations adhere to the governments regulations. then and only then can Ro legally sell his "shoe string spaghetti" to his eccentric customers. 



something to consider: compliance rules can come from multiple sources (like federal, state, or international agencies), cover different parts of the business (finance, data privacy, safety), and sometimes conflict with each other (for instance, one country might require Ro to store customer data locally, while another expects it to be accessible globally.). when this happens, Ro's organization must carefully balance and reconcile those requirements through governance and risk management; ensuring he stays compliant with all applicable rules without violating others. 


-----


internal compliance: sometimes Ro chooses to follow certain standards even though they are not required by gov, state, international org, or external vendor. usually this is for extra control means, security assurance means, or to plan for upcoming projects (like creating an online website where Ro's specialty "shoe food" can be ordered, thus needing PCI-DSS for processing and storing customer card information, etc., but Ro is just prepping for now.)


-----


Compliance Failures: Sometimes Ro's Shoe Food is not up to par with federal, state, or even internal policies and standards. Because of this Ro can face serious consequences, externally being government fines or penalties, internally being loss of customer trust or conflicting processes leading to inefficient operations. Since these risks can seriously damage the organization, most companies create a dedicated compliance or GRC function. this teams job is to monitor, enforce, and improve both internal and external compliance efforts so the business stays aligned with all required standards. 

in short, compliance protects the organization from legal, financial, and reputational hard; and failing to maintain it can weaken performance and credibility at every level.


