Value of Frameworks


*when an organization starts building a Governance, Risk, and Compliance (GRC) program, it usually begins by choosing an established framework that fits its business goals, industry, and risk appetite. this framework serves as a foundation giving structure and consistency to how the organization manages governance, risk, and compliance activities. 


Well Known Frameworks:
⦁	NIST (National Institute of Standards and Technology) — provides detailed, public-domain security and privacy control frameworks used widely in government and private sectors.

⦁	COBIT (Control Objectives for Information and Related Technology) — focuses more on aligning IT processes and governance with business objectives.

⦁	ISO/IEC (International Organization for Standardization / International Electrotechnical Commission) — offers globally recognized standards like ISO 27001 for information security management systems.


*each framework focuses on slightly different priorities but they all share common GRC principles, such as identifying controls, managing risk, and ensuring continuous improvement because the underlying concepts and controls overlap, organizations can often combine frameworks or map them to each other. 




