*the ISO 27001 certification process shares many structural similarities with the NIST Risk Management Framework (RMF), though they serve slightly different purposes:

⦁	Define Scope and Risk Assessment, ISMS scope definition/risk identification, prepare and categorize, identify all systems containing customer/supplier/and recipe data
⦁	Select Controls, statement of applicability (SoA), select, choose controls for data encryption/access management/and logging
⦁	Implement Controls, deploy ISMS policies and procedures, implement, apply encryption and role based access controls
⦁	Auditing/Certification, stage 1 & 2 third party audits, assess, auditors review logs and configurations for compliance
⦁	Certification Decision, achieve certification, authorize, AO authorizes system for operation once acceptable risk is verified
⦁	Continuous Improvement, surveillance and recertification audits, monitor, conduct internal audits and continuous security monitoring


both frameworks promote risk-based security, continuous monitoring, and traceability, but differ in purpose and audience. 

⦁	NIST RMF - internal, government driven framework for managing federal system risk (managing for U.S. agencies)
⦁	ISO 27001 - External, certification based framework for proving information security excellence to partners and customers


Ro uses both: ISO certification to build trust externally and NIST RMF concepts internally to structure consistent, risk informed decision making



